## 5. Architectural Quality: The "Long Run" View

| Quality Metric | Pure BDD | Pragmatic BDD | Why? |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Maintainability** | 🟢 High | 🟡 Medium | Pure BDD forces logic out of components. |
| **Living Docs** | 🟢 Yes | 🔴 No | Feature files are human-readable assets. |
| **Setup Complexity**| 🔴 High | 🟢 Low | Cucumber integration is a heavy lift. |
| **Dev Velocity** | 🟡 Medium | 🟢 High | Glue code slows down initial feature dev. |

## 6. The "Sovereign" Recommendation

If you want **Sovereign Quality** that lasts for years:

1.  **If the Feb 14 launch is immovable**: Stick to **Pragmatic BDD**. It gives you 80% of the benefit for 20% of the time.
2.  **If the Feb 14 launch is flexible**: Go with **Pure BDD (Cucumber)**. Starting with Gherkin files now sets a standard where the *Business Logic* is always clear, regardless of how much the code changes as we move toward v1.0 and SQLite+Drizzle.

**Final Analysis**: Going "Pure BDD" now is an investment. It will make the transition to SQLite/Drizzle *easier* because the feature files will stay the same even when the storage layer is ripped out and replaced.
